EXPERIMENT 8: Using a Pre-trained Model in Keras for Transfer Learning
Algorithm
1. Import the required TensorFlow/Keras libraries.
2. Load the CIFAR-10 image dataset.
3. Resize and normalize the images.
4. Load the pre-trained MobileNetV2 model without its top layer.
5. Freeze the pre-trained model layers.
6. Add a new classification layer for 10 classes.
7. Compile and train the model.
8. Evaluate the model using test data.
9. Display the test accuracy.
Code

import tensorflow as tf
from tensorflow.keras import layers, models

# Load CIFAR-10 dataset
(x_train, y_train), (x_test, y_test) = \
    tf.keras.datasets.cifar10.load_data()

# Resize images
x_train = tf.image.resize(x_train[:5000], (96, 96))
x_test = tf.image.resize(x_test[:1000], (96, 96))

y_train = y_train[:5000]
y_test = y_test[:1000]

# Load pre-trained MobileNetV2
base_model = tf.keras.applications.MobileNetV2(
    weights='imagenet',
    include_top=False,
    input_shape=(96, 96, 3)
)

# Freeze pre-trained layers
base_model.trainable = False

# Add classification layers
model = models.Sequential([
    base_model,
    layers.GlobalAveragePooling2D(),
    layers.Dense(10, activation='softmax')
])

# Compile model
model.compile(
    optimizer='adam',
    loss='sparse_categorical_crossentropy',
    metrics=['accuracy']
)

# Train model
model.fit(x_train, y_train, epochs=2, batch_size=32)

# Evaluate model
loss, accuracy = model.evaluate(x_test, y_test)

print("Test Accuracy:", round(accuracy, 4))

